        },
        "strategy": "direct",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional finite-time comparison of the adopted constant-mass relativistic Lorentz-force model with its formal Newtonian momentum-velocity replacement in prescribed SI electric and magnetic fields held fixed as the model parameter c tends to infinity. Both trajectories must exist on the same stated interval and stay in a common compact convex state region. This is not an empirical variation of nature's measured speed of light, a self-consistent Maxwell limit, a global-in-time claim, or a claim including self-field or radiation reaction.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-prescribed-force-local-motion",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Local massive motion in smooth orthogonal prescribed forces",
        "statement": "Let $(M,g,N)$ be a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian four-manifold and let $m,c>0$. Let $\\mathcal V\\subset TM$ be an open set of future timelike vectors, and let $f$ be a smooth vector field along the bundle projection $\\mathcal V\\to M$, with $f(p,V)\\in T_pM$. For the named massive test body, adopt the constant-mass force model of [[post-rpm-prescribed-force-law]]: $P=mU$, $g(U,U)=-c^2$, $D_\\tau P=f(x,U)$, and $g(U,f(x,U))=0$. If $\\tau$ is measured in seconds, then $P$ has SI units kilogram metres per second and $f$ has units newtons.\n\nFor each initial datum $(p_0,U_0)\\in\\mathcal V$ with $g_{p_0}(U_0,U_0)=-c^2$, there is a unique local future-timelike worldline satisfying the adopted equation. It remains on the future unit timelike shell and has a unique maximal solution on an open interval. In any chart-state domain where the coordinate force vector field is smooth, if a finite endpoint is approached while the state remains in a compact subset of that domain, the solution extends past the endpoint. No global lifetime or spacetime completeness is asserted.",
        "deps": [
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
          "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law",
          "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp",
          "lem-rpm-shell-force-ivp"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "def-rpm-spacetime-observer-and-worldline": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy": "formulation-prerequisite",
          "post-rpm-prescribed-force-law": "physical-assumption",
          "lem-rpm-shell-tangent-force-ivp": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-rpm-shell-force-ivp": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "ai-altered",
          "proof": "ai-altered"
        },
        "dependency_level": 7,
        "sources": {
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Gautam Iyer, Brief Notes on Introduction to Differential Equations, complete ODE notes",
              "url": "https://www.math.cmu.edu/~gautam/c/2026-632/notes/existence.html"
            }
          ]
        },
        "strategy": "direct",
        "physical_scope": "Conditional local dynamics of one massive test body with constant primitive mass on a prescribed smooth time-oriented Lorentzian background, under a separately adopted smooth external force orthogonal to its future unit velocity. It proves local motion and a chart-local compact-continuation criterion only; it makes no universal-force, global-completeness, self-force, or empirical claim.",
        "empirical_premises": []
      },
      {
        "id": "pthm-rpm-controlled-newtonian-reduction",
        "kind": "physics-theorem",
        "domain": "physics",
        "title": "Controlled Newtonian reductions for test motion",
        "statement": "The following are two separate conditional reductions, with no identification of the flat inertial quantities in clause (a) with the curved coordinate quantities in clause (b).\n\n**(a) Flat inertial kinematics.** In a named inertial chart of the flat massive-particle model [[post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics]], use the mechanical energy and momentum of [[def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy]]. Let $C>0$, $0<\\lambda\\le\\lambda_*$, and $\\rho=C^2\\lambda_*<1$. For any massive state with constant $m>0$, speed $v$, and $|v|\\le C\\sqrt\\lambda\\,c$, the bounds of [[lem-rpm-low-speed-uniform-remainders]] hold with $\\varepsilon=\\lambda$: the relativistic momentum and kinetic energy have the stated uniform Newtonian remainders, and the coordinate-time action density after subtracting the rest term differs from $\\frac12m|v|^2$ by at most $mC^4c^2\\lambda^2/[8(1-\\rho)^{3/2}]$. This is a kinematic assertion; it assumes no force law and proves no trajectory convergence.\n\n**(b) Curved weak-field free motion.** Adopt the separate massive test-particle action on a prescribed smooth background ([[post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action]]). Let the stationary weak-field metric family, scale $L$, spatial potential $\\Phi=\\varepsilon c^2\\varphi$, massive geodesic, compact spatial region, speed bound, Newtonian comparison solution, and Hessian bound satisfy exactly hypotheses (H1)–(H3) of [[thm-gr-weak-field-scaled-trajectory-limit]]. If the massive particle's free worldline is the geodesic of that theorem, then for the scaled trajectories $(X_\\varepsilon,W_\\varepsilon)$ and $(X_*,W_*)$,\n\n$$D(S):=\\|X_\\varepsilon(S)-X_*(S)\\|_2+\\|W_\\varepsilon(S)-W_*(S)\\|_2\\le\\bigl(D(0)+C_3\\varepsilon S_*\\bigr)e^{(1+K_2)S_*}\\qquad(0\\le S\\le S_*),$$\n\nwith $C_3,K_2,S_*$ exactly as in that theorem. In particular, for identical scaled initial data, $\\sup_{0\\le S\\le S_*}D(S)\\le C_3\\varepsilon S_*e^{(1+K_2)S_*}=O(\\varepsilon)$ on this fixed scaled interval. The corresponding coordinate-time horizon is $T_\\varepsilon=LS_*/(\\sqrt\\varepsilon c)$; this is not a large-time limit at fixed $\\varepsilon$.",
        "deps": [
          "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics",
          "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action",
          "def-rpm-mechanical-momentum-and-observer-energy",
          "pthm-rpm-free-massive-and-null-geodesics",
          "lem-rpm-low-speed-uniform-remainders",
          "thm-gr-weak-field-scaled-trajectory-limit"
        ],
        "dependency_roles": {
          "post-sr-massive-particle-dynamics": "physical-assumption",
          "post-rpm-massive-test-particle-action": "physical-assumption",
